        "title": "Pan-Slavic colors",
        "raw_score": 3.9835883784528003,
        "source": "kg"
      }
    },
    {
      "node_id": "kg_doc_2",
      "node_type": "kg",
      "content": "A tricolour or tricolor is a type of flag or banner design with a triband design which originated in the 16th century as a symbol of republicanism, liberty or indeed revolution. The flags of France, Italy, Romania, Mexico, and Ireland were all first adopted with the formation of an independent republic in the period of the French Revolution to the Revolutions of 1848, with the exception of the Irish tricolour, which dates from 1848 but was not popularised until the Easter Rising in 1916 and adopted in 1919.",
      "depth": 0,
      "parent_id": null,
      "value": 0.9999999915741536,
      "temperature": 0.9999999915741536,
      "expanded": false,
      "visit_count": 0,
      "persistent": true,
      "score_breakdown": {},
      "metadata": {
        "title": "Tricolour (flag)",
        "raw_score": 3.654714798236701,
        "source": "kg"
      }
    },
    {
      "node_id": "kg_doc_1",
      "node_type": "kg",
      "content": "The flag of the Kingdom of Thailand (Thai: ธงไตรรงค์ , \"Thong Trairong\", meaning \"tricolour flag”) shows five horizontal stripes in the colours red, white, blue, white and red, with the central blue stripe being twice as wide as each of the other four. The design was adopted on 28 September 1917, according to the royal decree issued by Rama VI that year. Since 2016, that day is a national holiday in Thailand celebrating the nation's flag.",
      "depth": 0,
      "parent_id": null,
      "value": 0.3432182602873537,
      "temperature": 0.3432182602873537,
      "expanded": false,
